   "source": [
    "### The Condemned: Death Sentences in the Modern Era (1976–2019)\n",
    "\n",
    "This dataset, compiled by *The Intercept*, includes all known individuals sentenced to death in active U.S. death penalty jurisdictions from July 2, 1976 (the date of *Gregg v. Georgia*) through November 30, 2019. It contains **7,335 individuals**, with each row representing one person sentenced to death during the modern death penalty era.\n",
    "\n",
    "As of 2019:\n",
    "- 1,448 individuals have been executed  \n",
    "- 2,752 remain on death row  \n",
    "- 3,135 have been removed from death row for reasons other than execution (commutations, exonerations, death in custody, etc.)\n",
    "\n",
    "This dataset allows us to examine how demographic, geographic, and temporal factors are associated with different death row outcomes.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Important Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "1. The dataset only includes individuals who received a death sentence. It does not include individuals charged with capital crimes who did not receive the death penalty, so we cannot analyze disparities in who receives a death sentence.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. The data were compiled from corrections departments, public defenders, advocacy groups, and independent trackers. Reporting standards may vary across states, which may introduce inconsistencies.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Individuals sentenced earlier have had more time for appeals, commutations, or execution. Recent sentences may appear more often as “currently on death row” due to time effects.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. The outcome categories are legally complex and require careful grouping for meaningful statistical analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n"
   ]
  },
